WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.280 --> 00:00:02.468
So this is really cool.

00:00:02.468 --> 00:00:09.805
What we've just found you can see is 
one, two, three, four, pieces of mammoth bone here.

00:00:09.805 --> 00:00:15.520
See these pieces are actually still frozen&nbsp;
in the permafrost we can't get them out at all.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.520 --> 00:00:17.820
Which means they're going to be really well&nbsp;
preserved.

00:00:17.820 --> 00:00:20.618
Just heard that big splash of water back there

00:00:20.618 --> 00:00:25.383
that means another hole's broken through.&nbsp;
Here comes the water we better get out of here

00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:33.440
Beth Shapiro came to genomics&nbsp;
through an interest in glaciers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.440 --> 00:00:37.120
ecosystems, and massive&nbsp;
changes in climate over time.

00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:45.280
I was super interested in the traces of the&nbsp;
glaciers that had extended across the continent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:48.545
and really thought that it would be amazing&nbsp;
if we could understand

00:00:48.545 --> 00:00:53.427
how that large-scale habitat shift had impacted ecosystems.

00:00:53.427 --> 00:00:57.840
This was in&nbsp;the early days of a new field called ancient DNA.

00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:07.278
Okay.

00:01:07.278 --> 00:01:13.436
It had only just been discovered that DNA is&nbsp;
preserved in the remains of organisms after they die,

00:01:13.436 --> 00:01:16.886
and it was just an amazing coincidence of&nbsp;opportunities.

00:01:16.886 --> 00:01:23.045
I ended up at Oxford University where one of the earliest ancient DNA labs was, and&nbsp;had an opportunity to join this field.

00:01:23.045 --> 00:01:27.794
I actually had no experience at all in molecular biology at&nbsp;
the time.

00:01:29.037 --> 00:01:33.065
In the early days we could only access DNA using PCR.

00:01:33.065 --> 00:01:38.417
Which meant that we needed to find&nbsp;
relatively long fragments of intact preserved DNA.

00:01:39.040 --> 00:01:42.720
It was really with the advent of next generation&nbsp;
sequencing technologies,

00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:48.903
where we could target and recover even the tiniest shortest&nbsp;
most broken fragments of DNA.

00:01:48.903 --> 00:01:52.694
Fragments that were as short as 25 or 30 bases long.

00:01:52.694 --> 00:01:57.348
That we could&nbsp;
really start to do population genetics at scale.

00:01:57.348 --> 00:02:01.718
With sequencing technologies Beth was able to&nbsp;
analyze enough data

00:02:01.718 --> 00:02:07.682
to work with older and less intact DNA samples going
further and further back&nbsp;in time.

00:02:08.356 --> 00:02:11.241
We had a mammoth fossil from Siberia

00:02:11.241 --> 00:02:14.471
and we extracted DNA from this mammoth fossil and&nbsp;
we learned

00:02:14.471 --> 00:02:17.937
by comparing each one of those tiny fragments of DNA

00:02:17.937 --> 00:02:21.605
to the online database of all&nbsp;the fragments of DNA that have ever been published,

00:02:21.605 --> 00:02:26.399
that only about half of the DNA that we were&nbsp;
recovering was mammoth DNA.

00:02:26.399 --> 00:02:32.489
The other half was things like bacteria or microbes other other fungi&nbsp;
that are that are in the ecosystem.

00:02:32.489 --> 00:02:40.237
We were super disappointed we thought oh no it's actually going&nbsp;
to take us so much more sequencing to get a whole&nbsp;&nbsp;mammoth genome.

00:02:40.237 --> 00:02:46.160
In fact what we now know is that&nbsp;
was an exceptionally well preserved specimen.

00:02:46.160 --> 00:02:52.031
That most of the specimens that we work with have&nbsp;
very little DNA that is endogenous to that animal.

00:02:53.280 --> 00:03:00.080
Gathering ancient DNA provides a unique insight&nbsp;
into climatic and ecological systems of the past,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.080 --> 00:03:03.840
giving scientists like Beth a&nbsp;
new window into human evolution.&nbsp;

00:03:05.200 --> 00:03:11.520
We now have genome-wide data from hundreds of&nbsp;
human remains from archaeological sites from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.520 --> 00:03:15.840
across the world and this has really&nbsp;
allowed us to begin to understand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:21.120
the fine scale picture of how humans moved&nbsp;
across the world how populations interacted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:27.280
with each other how environmental changes and the&nbsp;
neolithic, the use of domestic animals and plants&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.280 --> 00:03:32.811
really changed our lineage helped us to adapt to&nbsp;
become what we are today.

00:03:32.811 --> 00:03:36.359
Comparing the genomes of modern and archaic humans

00:03:36.359 --> 00:03:42.087
has also led to a&nbsp;deeper understanding of what 
makes us different from our ancient cousins.

00:03:42.087 --> 00:03:45.680
Well we've done some&nbsp; work using the neanderthal and denisovan genomes to

00:03:45.680 --> 00:03:48.065
compare all the genomes that are there and ask

00:03:48.065 --> 00:03:52.640
"are&nbsp;there regions of the genome where all 
humans share variants?"

00:03:52.640 --> 00:03:55.839
We've managed to narrow this down&nbsp;
to actually a really small portion of the genome,

00:03:55.839 --> 00:04:00.588
where where humans share some adaptive&nbsp;
change everybody has an adaptive change

00:04:00.588 --> 00:04:06.160
that neanderthals don't and the next phase of research&nbsp;
in human evolution is really i think going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.160 --> 00:04:11.440
focus in part on these mutations because these&nbsp;
are the clues to what it is that makes us human.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.880 --> 00:04:15.726
Big questions to answer with old bones.

00:04:16.735 --> 00:04:21.827
But Beth&nbsp;started on this road motivated 
by understanding the challenges of climate change.

00:04:21.827 --> 00:04:25.416
Can paleogenomics inform conservation?

00:04:26.205 --> 00:04:31.073
The past, one can think of as a completed evolutionary experiment.

00:04:31.073 --> 00:04:35.676
If we want to understand what happened during a period of rapid global warming

00:04:35.676 --> 00:04:39.713
we only have to look&nbsp;as far back as about 10 to 15 000 years ago

00:04:39.713 --> 00:04:44.518
when the planet went through an extremely rapid warming event out of the last ice age.

00:04:44.518 --> 00:04:50.790
And this rapid warming event was associated with&nbsp;
mass extinctions with reshuffling of ecosystems and communities,

00:04:50.790 --> 00:04:55.062
and it would be great if we could&nbsp;
use ancient DNA from the whole ecosystem,

00:04:55.062 --> 00:04:58.828
not just individual species but also from 
the plants and&nbsp;the microbes,

00:04:58.828 --> 00:05:07.115
to be able to better understand what it is that makes some communities more resilient in the face of this type of rapid climate change.

00:05:07.840 --> 00:05:13.840
From those first glimmers in frozen bones, ancient&nbsp;
genomics has exploded into a thriving field,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.840 --> 00:05:18.960
as new technology makes it possible to ask&nbsp;deeper questions about 
where we came from and where we are headed.

00:05:18.960 --> 00:05:23.841
Recently there's been a rapid&nbsp;
development of technologies

00:05:23.841 --> 00:05:28.498
to just get a piece of dirt, either from a core that you stick into a&nbsp;lake,

00:05:28.498 --> 00:05:33.970
or cores that you stick into frozen dirt or even just going 
and collecting sediments from&nbsp;the bottoms of caves.

00:05:33.970 --> 00:05:40.879
And being able to recover even population level information just from&nbsp;
DNA in that bit of dirt,

00:05:40.879 --> 00:05:45.647
and this is an amazing resource for looking at the entire community

00:05:45.647 --> 00:05:49.119
not just the individual whose bone happened to be preserved,

00:05:49.119 --> 00:05:54.917
but the plants and the animals and&nbsp;the microbes everything that 
was interacting to make that community what it was.

00:05:54.917 --> 00:06:01.613
This is how we're&nbsp;really going to begin to get insights into 
what allows these ecosystems to be more resilient.

00:06:01.613 --> 00:06:05.217
These insights that will be helpful as we begin to make

00:06:05.217 --> 00:06:10.925
more informed decisions about how to&nbsp;
protect and preserve ecosystems in the present day. 

